July 2013 the European Space Agency  cryosat-2 satellite uses radar  technology to build 3d maps of  Antarctica it discovers something no one  was expecting to see a giant hole in the  ice the hole appears east of the Ross  Ice Shelf it captures the attention of  glaciologist kelly brunt the depression  in the ice she's about three times the  size of the island of Manhattan so it's  a very big hole it's about 150 to 200  feet deep so we want to know what is  going on here scientists attempt to find  a natural explanation for the disturbing  phenomenon  they first consider that warm ocean  currents could have created the hole by  melting the ice from below but there's a  problem with this theory the giant hole  is miles from the ocean scientists now  believe there's something ancient  lurking beneath the ice that created the  hole in the geological past Antarctica  actually had a different climate perhaps  1015 million years ago there was an  opportunity for running water to it to  have existed on the surface that water  has got trapped as Lakes a couple of  miles sometimes down beneath the ice  sheets those lakes have been trapped  there for millions of years  these ancient subterranean bodies of  water are known as ghost lakes we call  these Lakes ghost lakes because very  invisible to us until the something  happens and they reveal themselves  aerial scans using  ice-penetrating radar reveal an anomaly  almost a mile and a half beneath the  surface directly beneath the hole it  looks exactly like a ghost lake  the waters entirely at the base of the  ice sheets  despite being thousands of feet beneath  the ice the water in the lakes remains  liquid and it can move from one ghost  lake to another these leaks spread  throughout the continent and they're  interconnected as one lake drains it  could fill another lake downstream and  as water in that system in a lake moves  from one place to another the ice sheet  loses that pressure that's holding it up  and it tends to drop it appears that the  movement of the underground water caused  a slump in the surface ice creating the  giant hole  you
 

